      "text": "The ellipse is centered at the origin with the major axis along the x-axis and the minor axis along the y-axis. The lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes are 5 and 4, respectively. The distance from the center to each focus is given by \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\\). The foci are located at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\nA circle centered at one of the foci with radius \\(r\\) is tangent to the ellipse. The circle is tangent to the ellipse at some point on the ellipse, and the distance from the focus to this point is equal to the radius \\(r\\). This means that the point of tangency lies on the line segment connecting the focus to the point where the circle is tangent to the ellipse.\n\nThe distance from a focus to the center of the ellipse is \\(c = 3\\). The distance from the focus to the point of tangency on the ellipse (which is the radius of the circle) is \\(r\\). The point of tangency forms a right triangle with the radius as one leg, the distance from the center of the ellipse to the focus as the other leg, and the semi-major axis as the hypotenuse. The equation of this right triangle is:\n\n\\[\nr^2 + 3^2 = 5^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\n\\[\nr^2 + 9 = 25\n\\]\n\n\\[\nr^2 = 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\nr = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
